  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le \u03d5\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
